1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaghri_Beg '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Chaghri Beg'''[1] (Turkish: Çağrı Bey, full name: Abu Suleiman Dawud Chaghri-Beg ibn Mikail) (989 - 1060), Da'ud b. Mika'il b. Saljuq,[2] also spelled Chaghri, was the co-ruler of the early Seljuq empire. The name Chaghri is Tu
2 CONC rkic (Çağrı in modern Turkish) and literally means "small falcon", "merlin".[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Background
2 CONT * 2 Biography
2 CONT * 3 Death
2 CONT * 4 Legacy
2 CONT * 5 Issue
2 CONT * 6 References
2 CONT
2 CONT =Background=
2 CONT
2 CONT Chaghri and his brother Tughril were the sons of Mikail and the grand-sons of Seljuq. The Great Seljuq Empire was named after the latter, who was a Turkic clan leader either in Khazar[4] or Oghuz states. In the early years of the 
2 CONC 11th century, they left their former home and moved near the city of Jend (now a village) by the Syr Darya river, where they accepted the suzerainty of the Karakhanids in Transoxania (roughly modern Uzbekistan and southern Kazakhs
2 CONC tan). After the defeat of the Karakhanids by Ghaznavids, they were able to gain independence.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known of Chaghri and Tughril's lives until 1025. Both were raised by their grandfather Seljuq until they were fifteen and fought with Ali Tigin Bughra Khan, a minor Kara Khanid noble, against Mahmud of Ghazni.[5] Th
2 CONC e earliest records about Chaghri are about his expeditions in Eastern Anatolia. Although Ghaznavid governor chased him from his home to Anatolia, he was able to raid the Byzantine forts in Eastern Anatolia.[6] However according to
2 CONC  Claude Cahen this was highly improbable and of legend.[7] From 1035 to 1037 Chaghri and Tughril fought against Mas'ud I of Ghazni. Chaghri captured Merv (an important historical city now in Turkmenistan).[8] Between 1038 and 1040
2 CONC  Chaghri fought against the Ghaznavids, usually with hit and run manoeuvres. Major clash was the Battle of Dandanaqan. Tughril was rather hesitant and suggested to continue with the hit and run operations. But Chagri preferred to 
2 CONC fight and he commanded the Seljuk army.[9] At Dandanaqan, the Seljuks defeated the numerically superior Gaznavid army. A kurultai was held after the battle, by which empire was divided between the two brothers. While Tughril reign
2 CONC ed in the west (comprising modern western Iran, Azerbaijan and Iraq), Chaghri reigned in eastern Iran, Turkmenistan, and Afghanistan. Chaghri later also captured Balkh (in modern North Afghanistan). In 1048, he conquered Kerman in
2 CONC  South Iran and, in 1056, the Sistan region (south east Iran).[10] After the Seljuqs had gained more influence over the Abbasid caliphate, Chaghri married his daughter, Arslan Khatun Khadija, to the caliph Al-Qa'im in 1056.[11][12
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Death=
2 CONT
2 CONT Chaghri died in Sarakhs, in North-eastern Iran. The historical sources do not agree on the exact date of his death: years 1059, 1060, 1061 and 1062 were proposed. But it is purported that numismatics can be used to determine the e
2 CONC xact death date. Coins were minted in the name of Chaghri up to 1059 and in the name of his son Kavurt after 1060, so Chaghri's death can be ascribed more probably to 1059.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Legacy=
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike later Ottoman practice, in earlier Turkic tradition, brothers usually participated in government affairs.(Bumin - İstemi in the 6th century, Bilge Khan – Kultegin in the 8th century are notable examples.) Tughril and Chaghr
2 CONC i as well as some other members of the family participated in the foundation of the empire. Although Tughril gained the title "sultan", it was Chaghri’s sons who continued it afterwards.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chaghri had six sons and four daughters. Among his sons, Alp Arslan became the sultan in 1064. All the remaining members of the Great Seljuq Empire were from Chaghri’s lineage. Another son, Kavurt, became the governor of Kerman (w
2 CONC hich later on became fully independent); a third son, Yaquti, became the governor of Azerbaijan.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Issue=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alp_Arslan Alp Arslan]
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qavurt Kavurt]
2 CONT * Yaquti
2 CONT * Suleiman
2 CONT * Bahram-Shah
2 CONT * Ilyas
2 CONT * Uthman
2 CONT * Khadija Arslan-Khatun
2 CONT * Gouhar Khatun
2 CONT * Safiye
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Chaghri Beg (Seljuq ruler) Encyclopædia Britannica
2 CONT #  'Izz al-D in Ibn al-Athir, The Annals of the Saljuq Turks, transl. D.S. Richards, ed. Carole Hillenbrand, (Routledge, 2002), 302.
2 CONT # "ČAḠRĪ BEG DĀWŪD" Encyclopædia Iranica
2 CONT # Arthur Koestler: The thirteenth Tribe (translated by Belkıs Çorakçı), Say, İstanbul, 1984, p.164
2 CONT # Caghri-Beg, Cl. Cahen, The Encyclopaedia of Islam, Vol. II, ed. B.Lewis, C. Pellat and J. Schacht, (E.J. Brill, 1991), 4.
2 CONT # Prof. Yaşar Yüce-Prof. Ali Sevim: Türkiye tarihi Cilt I, AKDTYKTTK Yayınları, İstanbul, 1991 p 28-29
2 CONT # Caghri beg, Claude Cahen, The Encyclopaedia of Islam, Vol. II, 4, 5; "Tradition gives here an account of a highly improbable escapade of Caghri-Beg in Armenia.", "On the legendary escapade of Caghri in Armenia, the article of Ib
2 CONC rahim Kafesoglu, "Dogu Anadoluya ilk selcuklu adini", in "Fuad Koprulu Armagam", 1953, and my discussion with him in JA 1954, 275 ff. and 1956, 129 ff."
2 CONT # 'Izz al-D in Ibn al-Athir, The Annals of the Saljuq Turks, 36.
2 CONT # Ümit Hassan (ed.Sina Akşin) Türkiye Tarihi I, CemYayınevi, İstanbul,2009, ISBN 975-406-563-2 p. 167
2 CONT # History page (Turkish)[dead link]
2 CONT # The Political and Dynastic History of the Iranian World, C.E. Bosworth, The Cambridge History of Iran, Vol. 5, ed. J. A. Boyle, (Cambridge University Press, 1968), 48.
2 CONT # Dailamīs in Central Iran: The Kākūyids of Jibāl and Yazd, C. E. Bosworth, Iran, Vol. 8, (1970), 86.
2 CONT # [http://dergiler.ankara.edu.tr/dergiler/18/32/241.pdf '''A paper on Chaghri's death date''']
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Chaghri Beg's later life and death are mentioned on pages 129-130 in [https://books.google.com/books?id=BAN6ONlDkgIC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false '''The Annals of the Saljuq Turks: Select
2 CONC ions from Al-Kamil Fi'l-Ta'rikh of Ibn Al-Athir'''], translated by D.S. Richards.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peace was established this year between King Ibrahim and Da’ud ibn Mikha’il ibn Saljuq, the lord of Khurasan, on condition that each  of them should keep what he held and abandon opposition to the other’s rule. The reason for this
2 CONC  was that the wise men on both sides considered [the situation] and realised that neither of the two rulers was able to take what the other held, and that the only result would be expenditure of money, exhaustion of the troops, pl
2 CONC undering of the land and loss of life. So they worked for peace, and an accord was reached and oaths sworn. Copies were drawn up, and the people were delighted and rejoiced at the prospect of prosperity.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rajab of this year [13 August-11 Septemener 1059] there died Chagri Beg Da’ud ibn Mikha’il ibn Saljuq, the brother of Sultan Tughril Beg. It is said that his death was in Safar [4]52 [7 March-4 APril 1060]. He was about seventy
2 CONC  years old. He was the lord of khurasan and the rival andopponent of the House of Sabuktegin, and the defender of Khurasan againstthe,. When he died, his son Alp Arslan became ruler of Khurasan after him. Da’ud left a number of ma
2 CONC le children, including the Sultan Alp Arslan, Yaquti, Sulayman, and Qavurt Beg. After [the death of] his brother Da’ud, the Sultan Tughril Beg married the mother of Sulayman, and named him as his successor. What happened to him we
2 CONC  shall relate later.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Chaghri Beg] was generous and just, a good ruler, one who was conscious of God Almighty’s favours to him and grateful for them. An example of this is that he sent to his brother Tughril Beg by Abd al-Samad, the Cadi of Sarakhs, s
2 CONC aying ‘I have heard that you are ruining the lands you have conquered and taken, whose inhabitants have fled them. This is manifestly in opposition to what God Almighty orders for His servants and His lands. You also know what thi
2 CONC s involves in the way of evil repute and alienation of one’s subjects. You are aware that we met our enemies with 30 men, when they were 300 strong, but we overcame them. We led 300 when they were 3,000 strong, and we overcame the
2 CONC m. We had 3,000, when they were 30,000 strong, and we repelled them. Once in the past we fought Shah Malik, when he commanded numerous hordes of [our] enemies, and we crushed him. We took his kingdom in Khwarizm and he fled 500 le
2 CONC agues away from us, and yet we seized and captured, then slew him. We conquered the kingdoms of Khurasan, Tabaristan and Sijistan, and became powerful kings after we had been weak subjects. The favors God has shown us do not deser
2 CONC ve to be repaid in this manner.’ Tughril Beg said, ‘Say to him in reply, “My brother, you took Khurasan as a flourishing land, and you have ruined it, although with the establishment of your position it was your duty to make it fl
2 CONC ourish. I have come to a land ruined by those who preceded me and destroyed by those before me. I am incapable of making it flourish while enemies encompass it. Necessity leads armies to tramp th[r]ough it and it is impossible to 
2 CONC stop them harming it.”’
2 CONT
2 CONT He possessed many virtues which we have passed over for fear of prolixity.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaghri_Beg Chaghri Beg[1] (Turkish: Çağrı Bey, full name: Abu Suleiman Dawud Chaghri-Beg ibn Mikail) (989–1060), Da'ud b. Mika'il b. Saljuq,[2] also spelled Chaghri, was the co-ruler of the early Sel
2 CONC juq empire. The name Chaghri is Turkic (Çağrı in modern Turkish) and literally means "small falcon", "merlin".[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Chaghri and his brother Tughril were the sons of Mikail and the grandsons of Seljuq. The Great Seljuq Empire was named after the latter, who was a Turkic clan leader either in Khazar[4] or Oghuz states. In the early years of the 1
2 CONC 1th century, they left their former home and moved near the city of Jend (now a village) by the Syr Darya river, where they accepted the suzerainty of the Karakhanids in Transoxania (roughly modern Uzbekistan and southern Kazakhst
2 CONC an). After the defeat of the Karakhanids by Ghaznavids, they were able to gain independence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known of Chaghri and Tughril's lives until 1025. Both were raised by their grandfather Seljuq until they were fifteen and fought with Ali Tigin Bughra Khan, a minor Kara Khanid noble, against Mahmud of Ghazni.[5] Th
2 CONC e earliest records of Chaghri concern his expeditions in Eastern Anatolia.
