1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVdW8EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6
2 CONC AEwAw#v=onepage&q=Aka&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K.S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT Chagri Beg’s daughter Arslan Khatun, who was first married to the caliph al-Qa’im, was married to the Kakuyid of Yazd Ala al-Daula Ali b. Abi Nasur Faramarz in 469/1076-7, by which time al-Qa’im had died. There were other marriage
2 CONC s between the Saljuqs and the Kakuyids of Yazd. Sitara, the sister of Mohammad b. Malik Shah and Sanjar, was married to Ala al-Daula Garchasp b. Ali b. Faramarz.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from page 137-139 of [https://books.google.com/books?id=BAN6ONlDkgIC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false '''The Annals of the Saljuq Turks: Selections from Al-Kamil Fi'l-Ta'rikh of Ibn Al-Athir'
2 CONC ''], translated by D.S. Richards:
2 CONT
2 CONT [Tughril Beg] assembled his court and told them that his ambition had soared to union with this lady of the Prophet's lineage, and that he had attained what no ruler before him had attained. He commanded Amid al-Mulk, the vizier, 
2 CONC to go with Arslan Khatun, the caliph's wife, and to provide her with 100,000 dinars for the payment, and suitable jewels and such like. He also dispatched Faramurz ibn Kakuya and other leading emirs and notables of Rayy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When he had come to the Imam al-Qa'im bi-Amr Allah, escorted the Lady, the caliph's wife, to her residence, and reported his presence and that of his party, and told how the proposed alliance stood. The caliph, however, refused to
2 CONC  agree to it, and said, 'If we are not permitted to decline, we shall leave Baghdad...' 
2 CONT
2 CONT ...Tughril Beg demanded that his niece, the caliph's wife, be returned to him."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Arslan Khatun is mentioned again on page 142 of [https://books.google.com/books?id=BAN6ONlDkgIC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false '''The Annals of the Saljuq Turks: Selections from Al-Kamil Fi
2 CONC 'l-Ta'rikh of Ibn Al-Athir'''], translated by D.S. Richards:
2 CONT
2 CONT This year in Rabi' I [March 1063] the sultan travelled from Baghdad to the Uplands. He came to Rayy, accompanied by the Lady Arslan, his niece and wife of the caliph, because she had complained of the caliph's abandonment of her. 
2 CONC He took her with him therefore. However, he became ill and died on Friday, 8 Ramadan [Friday = 5 September 1063]. He was approximately seventy years old and without issue, never having produced any child.
